[Music]  hi my name is kelly bajanke and i'm an  assistant professor in the department of  neurosurgery at baylor college of  medicine  i wanted to start by giving a big thank  you to the organizers for putting all  this together and for the invitation to  speak today  i have the privilege of sharing with you  a set of experiments we've undertaken to  leverage the insights of stereotactic  eeg  in order to inform deep brain  stimulation therapy for treatment  resistant depression  the first part of this talk will  describe the design and philosophy of  the study the second part we'll discuss  the intracranial monitoring period and  our approach to characterization of the  trd network  and then the third part discusses our  efforts at mapping the depression  circuitry using multimodal fusion across  imaging and electrophysiology  so just to give a brief background there  are several brain targets undergoing  active investigation for the treatment  of treatment resistant depression  they're indicated here the current study  focuses on the first two the central  capsule ventral striatum target and the  subclosal cingulate target  we're investigating these targets  simultaneously  so implanting bilateral leads in the  central capsule ventral striatum and  also bilaterally in the subclosal  cingulate at the same time  during that surgery we're also placing  10  stereotactic eeg electrodes which are  temporary so they're kept in the brain  for the intracranial monitoring period  and then at the end of that period  they're removed  and the patient begins an open label  deep brain stimulation  therapeutic optimization phase  so just to say a little bit more about  the stereo eg approach so this approach  is borrowing from a routine diagnostic  practice in the surgical treatment of  epilepsy  wherein  temporary depth electrodes are implanted  in the brain connected to recording  equipment and then patients are  monitored during a hospital stay that  typically ranges from one to three weeks  recordings are then studied to provide  guidance on the neural underpinnings of  the patient's disorder and to guide the  next steps of treatment  the  intracranial electrodes themselves are  not designed to provide treatment  directly  so we're using them for the same ends in  treatment resistant depression  um this is the structure of the study  the study is led by three pis senior  chef nader peradian and wayne goodman  samir and and nader are the  neurosurgeons at baylor college of  medicine in houston in  university of texas southwestern in  dallas  dr goodman  is the chair of the psychiatry  department at baylor college of medicine  we receive psychiatric support from  sanjay matthew and kayla bailey  i direct the neuroscience efforts  particularly focusing on the  intracranial monitoring period at baylor  we receive engineering support from  brown university assessment tool support  from  from upmc and from the university of  chicago and then device support from  boston scientific and we have also a  critical collaboration with cameron  mcintyre and angela naker at duke  so i'd like to show this video clip to  demonstrate the process a little bit the  study enrolls patients with treatment  resistant depression that has failed to  respond to a variety of prior adequate  therapies we study the underlying brain  state in these cases and then we apply  initial stimulation through the deep  brain stimulation leads indicated here  in yellow  and characterize the change in network  activity  as you can see in the recording here  indicated in yellow  the change in the network that is  directly evoked by the simulation and  then also the more passive change in  just the actual resting native  electrophysiological state of the brain  after the patient has received  stimulation pulses  after 10 days of study as you saw the  seg electrodes come out the pulse  generate pulse generators are implanted  subclavicularly and the patient begins a  chronic outpatient dbs treatment  optimization phase  so in this study we have two main goals  we want to understand the  pathophysiological state of treatment  resistant depression then we want to  take a data-driven approach to the  development of stimulation programming  parameters that are individualized to  these patients  from their own brain recording  a little more information about the  stereo eeg sampling so these electrodes  are implanted broadly we sort of  designed the study that we had some  brain regions that we were interested in  the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex the  intermedial prefrontal cortex orbital  frontal both medial and lateral  the dorsal anterior cingulate cortex and  the amygdala  this approach has evolved quite a bit  through our collaboration with dr  mcintyre  at duke so here you can see um  our use in the study  of the microsoft hololens platform and  the what we call the hollow seed  approach which is developed by dr  mcintyre  and his lab at now at duke  so what you can see projected in the  middle of the space here is the  patient's actual  t1 brain scan and the patient-specific  probabilistic tactography the the white  matter fibers of the patient's brain  itself  so everyone is standing in the room  together  wearing the same holographic  headsets and then we're able to see and  manipulate this three-dimensional brain  volume together and basically step  through  the surgery  virtually to plan it  so we use the patient-specific  tactography to define the implant  targets using methods that have been  existing in the literature developed by  dr mcintyre and dr mayberg at emory  for the fcc we are targeting the  confluence of  some major fiber systems including the  forceps minor the cingulum bundle and  uncertainty fasciculus and then we're  using a similar but like new approach  to define the vcbs target that is based  on maximizing engagement to the fibers  of the anterior limb that innervate the  orbital frontal and ventral medial  prefrontal cortices  and this method  has just recently been published in  thanks at all 2022  and then  in the holographic planning space we're  able to then use  dr mcintyre's biophysical modeling of  the volume of tissue activated  to identify for example if we put the  scc leads in this patient in this spot  and then we turn on the stimulation  what are the fibers that are going to  intersect with that volume of  stimulation then we can project those  fibers out and then  in patient-specific space  plan the stereo eeg trajectory to  traverse the terminations of that fiber  system that we anticipate to be  modulating and so that's the way that  the  final placement of the seg electrodes is  implemented for each patient  so as a summary of the philosophy of the  study we believe that network disorders  like psychiatric disorders demand a  network-based therapy  and the use of epilepsy-style  intracranial recordings provide a unique  perspective into network activity and  help us characterize the effects of  stimulation  individualization of imaging and  electrophysiological analyses will  hopefully drive  improved outcomes for patients  okay so  the next part of the talk i'd like to  focus on the 10-day intracranial study  phase  so after implantation patients are moved  to the neurophysiological monitoring  unit  here you can see a sketch of how the  time runs where we start out on days one  and two with pretty basic brain  recordings at rest and during behavioral  tasks that are related to depression  before we begin an exploration of  stimulation parameter space starting  with short stimulation durations at  single contacts on individual leads and  slowly working up toward longer and  multi-lead stimulation paradigms over on  days seven and eight and culminating  with repeats of this cognitive task  during task specific individualized  stimulation parameter application  which lets us evaluate our capacity to  differentially modulate portions of the  network corresponding with different  aspects of the patient's depressive  phenomenology  in the next slides i'll show some  examples of our methods for decoding the  neural state that corresponds with the  affect of neuroscience tasks that we run  and for developing targeted approaches  to stimulation parameter recommendation  but first i just wanted to spend a  couple of minutes  talking about the overall outcome of the  case in our first patient whose data is  displayed here as you can see the  patient underwent increasing stimulation  over the period of the intracranial  monitoring phase and over the course of  this this instantaneous depression scale  rating plotted here in blue  dropped over time with the top  stimulation parameters evoking  self-reported serenity calm and focus  increased energy and social engagement  and in several conditions the patient  remarked that if he felt this way all  the time he would be much better at work  and in his personal life  the patient has gone on to a phenomenal  clinical response and has demonstrated a  discontinuation response consistent with  dbs efficacy in treating his  depression a little bit more information  about our stimulation parameter space  exploration so  during the intracranial monitoring phase  we apply  single pulse stimulation to characterize  the structure of the network  the longest stimulation that we do is  actually a 16-hour long experiment  spread over multiple days in which we  sample across um  three frequencies and three levels of  pulse width charge balanced with  amplitude and across seven current  directions we do this individually for  each of the four dbs leads  and when i say current directions i'm  referring to  the  the fact that we're using the boston  scientific cartesia lead system which is  a segmented steerable dbs lead system so  we're able to sample from the deepest  and the most superior contacts as a  solid ring and a bullet tip respectively  and then the middle two contacts are  actually segmented such that we're able  to direct current for example to the  anterior facing stack the medial facing  stack with the lateral facing stack  we're interested in this because it  allows us greater refinement in the  white matter fibers that we think we're  engaging and potentially with  directionality  to the orientation of the applied field  that then could correspond with  different engagement of different fiber  systems  then we move into a 15 second  stimulation experiment  which is designed in a slightly  different way of a different purpose  than the one second stimulation one  second is  randomized to the level of trials so  each applied one second stimulation  parameter combination is different from  the one that will follow it this is to  counterbalance against temporal drift in  the recording  and potential follow-on effects from one  stimulation to the next  the 15-second experiment is actually  designed to help us quantify um  behavioral effects of different  stimulation parameter combinations and  so for this we do 15 seconds of  stimulation on followed by five seconds  of stimulation off  it starts with single lead and then  builds up into a multi-lead stimulation  approach  and after the application of  10 trials of these 15 seconds like in  each parameter combination there's a  brief psychiatric interview so during  the duration of this experiment the  psychiatrist is sitting with the patient  and after the  the period of the stimulation the  psychiatrist does a brief  interview just asking did you notice any  change  do you like how is your mood how is your  energy um using the words that are  specific to the way the patient  describes their own phenomenology  and then toward the end we do a longer  stimulation these are um  multi-lead for a longer duration to  allow the patient to sort of get a sense  of the feeling of stimulation in a more  chronic way as a preview of what they  might expect in their outpatient  programming phase  so one of the tasks that we use in the  intracranial monitoring period is called  the affective bias task the task in  which the patients rate the intensity  and valence of presented emotional  facial expressions  we use this task  to characterize at the level of each  electrode as shown here in these  spectrograms  the response of neural activity across  frequency space and we can see here  differential responses to happy versus  sad facial expressions where this on the  left side indicated at the top with  positive is the  the neural response to happy faces and  on the right panel of the spectrogram  is the neural response to sad faces  we can examine then all the electrodes  responses together to see spatial  patterns indicated in the right-hand  column  spatial patterns across the network and  this task is showing robust patterned  responses distinguishing between the  viewing of negatively valence and  positively valence facial expressions  these data and others are then used to  build neural state signatures to compare  against the neural responses to  stimulation  we're also able to use the property of  affective biasing itself  in the study so just to speak a little  more about that affective bias refers to  the phenomenon wherein people interpret  emotional stimuli in a manner consistent  with their own internal emotional state  so patients with depression when they're  asked to rate an array of emotional  faces they'll rate them more negatively  than those same patients would rate them  after an effective round of  antidepressant therapy  and they also rate the faces more  negatively than a normative  population of healthy control  participants and so we're able to use  this response property of affective  biasing  and explore it as a function of valence  and intensity of the stimuli as well as  using it as a readout of the  neuromodulatory impact of stimulation on  mood  um another application of the affective  bias task is looking at um the it as a  network quantification um so looking at  alpha band and theta band activity on  the first day that the task is  implemented prior to any stimulation  being applied  and then looking at it again after the  accumulation of multiple days of  long-form stimulation so this is day two  of the intraoperative period versus day  nine  and so here you can see  plotted in the red dots um this  indicates that there's greater power in  these indicated contacts for positive  faces  um and the blue dots indicate greater  power in response to viewing negative  faces  so  we're interpreting this as some evidence  that just the exposure to stimulation  itself may be shifting the brain's  response to affective stimuli  um  sort of wholesale on the on the global  scale  another task that we use to characterize  the neural state is the emotional brain  behavior quantification where patients  view emotional video clips and rate  their valence intensity and familiarity  again we're able to record across the  network during the task  and we can  in the video task we can decode the time  component in panel c  against the ongoing action  of the video clip in panel d so here you  can see the um in panel d the ratings of  the  ongoing action in the video clip are  getting happier corresponding with that  same time course in panel c  theta power is actually increasing in  the brain network that we're monitoring  so  we're able to then use these data to  build a classifier which we've been  able to use to distinguish between the  neural response to happy and sad  video clips and so far these neural  responses and the classifier shows good  discriminability  the last part of the approach i'd like  to describe is one of now several  methods that we've developed  for using the intracranial  electrophysiology to describe to drive  individualized stimulation parameter  selection  so the way the process works kind of  globally is that we characterize power  in each electrode in each stimulation  condition  so we define a desired state indicated  in panel one in the yellow  this can be a naturalistically observed  positive mood state  it can be  the viewing of positive video clips the  viewing of happy facial expressions and  the bias task  the receipt of a reward during our  probabilistic reward task  and so we define anyway a desired state  and characterize  the  power across canonical frequency bands  in all of our recording electrodes  during that desired state and then we  search across the neural responses in  that frequency band  to all of the applications of  stimulation that we have tried during  the stimulation parameter search and  then we're just ranking them in terms of  the degree of match between the matching  the correlation matrices  by  condition and by frequency to optimize  the match between the desired neural  state and the  neural state evoked by the stimulation  parameters and then we iteratively fit a  generalized linear model to optimize the  stimulation parameters to evoke that  state so this is a way of looking at the  strongest contributions of the model  where we're able to see  the highest beta weights the sort of  strongest contributions to the  predictivity of the correlation matrix  between the desired state and the  stimulation of hope state strongest  contribution is driven by electrode  contact configuration so which of the  actual electrodes  on the dbs lead were stimulated is the  strongest predictor and the second  strongest predictor is the frequency  with um smaller contributions from pulse  width and amplitude  and then an exploratory approach that  we're using um is dimensionality  reduction to try to  interpret the neural state so we're able  to kind of plot and see  for example defining a  healthy versus a thick resting state and  then plotting  the  the neural features corresponding with  the different stimulations that we apply  you can see with the right scc medial  stimulation we're not really changing  the neural state very much from the sick  resting state whereas when we stimulate  the right scc in the more lateral  contact  we're evoking kind of a separate state  altogether  so it's  physically sort of discriminable and  distinct from the well state but it's  also separate from the sick state  okay so now changing gears a little bit  again to the our multimodal  investigation of mechanisms so this is  looking at  methods for pairing the  electrophysiology with the neuroimaging  data to tell us more about the structure  of the network  so this is the work of josh atkinson a  postdoc in samir chef's lab and evie  solaki a postdoc in native paradians lab  so in this experiment we're applying  single pulses of stimulation to dbs  target structures  and we understand that the application  of single pulses generates axonal  depolarization and triggers action  potentials to propagate through the  affected circuitry and result in  neurotransmission events in the cortex  and the gray matter structures  um  so where we understand that the single  pulse stimulation is able to give rise  to both orthodomic so liking to along  the main axis of the axon and also  antidramic so backwards toward the cell  body  of  of any axon that is being engaged  and that it's able to drive firing  through multi-synaptic circuits  and that this engagement may not map  completely onto the  properties that are being modeled by  diffusion tensor imaging this is  important because diffusion tensor  imaging is being used  um broadly by the field for  stimulation targeting  for um surgical lead placement um  including by our group but then  also  for understanding what we think the  effect of stimulation um and dbs is on  the network we're only through diffusion  tensor imaging able to quantify  the sort of monosynaptic like  structurally  integrated white matter fibers  that  may not  transmit through to the next level  okay so the way that we are approaching  this you can see that we can use the  patient-specific probabilistic  tractography to visualize the structural  connectivity  that's expected from the just from the  patient's dti so here you can see in  panel a the volume of tissue activated  engages the forceps minor in the  cingulum bundle then when we implant an  seg electrode in the terminations of the  forceps minor  we're able to actually do the experiment  apply a single pulse on the implanted  dbs lead and then record from  the  the seg lead that's in the termination  area of the forceps minor and that's  what you're seeing actually then tested  in panel b you can see the stimulation  artifact is very quick and then we're  seeing a positive going  waveform that starts around 19  milliseconds and then a negative going  phase around 40 milliseconds so this is  the evo potential that is generated and  recorded in basically orbital frontal  cortex following stimulation to this  structure  and then panel c and d are showing  our ways of  measuring the prediction  of the network from the diffusion tensor  imaging where red is indicating the  number of streamlines anticipated to  reach from the stimulation volume to the  recording positions  and then panel d is the actual  evo potential that we were recording so  there's quite a bit of discontinuity  between the two actually where we see  much stronger responses  in sort of orbital frontal cortex  and  sort of a lack of correspondence to  other structures that were anticipated  to be  structurally connected to this from the  diffusion tensor imaging  and then in panels e and f um it's  another way of looking at this so across  the top the labels are indicating the  listening areas basically so the orbital  frontal cortices medial and lateral the  ventrimedial prefrontal cortex amygdala  anterior cingulate and the dorsal  prefrontal cortex you can see again sort  of  in the upper panels the streamline  predictions and in the lower panel f  is the actual evoked responses where  blue is an evoked response and white is  at the absence of an evoked response  and here you can see strong  correspondence in the orbital frontal  cortexes in the ventrimedial prefrontal  cortex and then a lack of correspondence  actually in the anterior cingulate where  it was  anticipated to have  streamlined connectivity but then we  don't see the corresponding  evoked potentials  this is interesting to us  so we've now replicated this across our  first two patients  and we're able to dig into this  phenomenon a little bit more  so just to explain this figure  a little more the labels across the top  are the same sort of listening areas of  the seg electrodes and then the columns  on the left hand side are the different  applications of stimulation the upper is  the subclosal cingulate and the lower is  the ventral capsule central striatum  stimulation target  the upper half of each is the left and  the lower half is the right  and then panel a  in each is the streamlines where warmer  colors indicate greater predicted  streamline connectivity from the  simulation volume to the listening  volume  and b is showing the actual evoked  potentials from stimulation that we  applied at each of the stimulation  combinations shown in the um  in the index here  and  so there's a lot to look at but the gist  here for what really stood out to us and  looking at it is again this anterior  cingulate thing right where the fcc is  anticipated to  generate engagement in the anterior  cingulate based on the streamlines and  we see really very little of that  bear itself out in panel b  with the evoked potentials it's um  really fairly minimal whereas sort of  the opposite is observed with the  ventral capsule central striatum there's  nothing in our  in our streamline models or anything  that would predict that we would have  anterior cingulate engagement and yet we  actually have fairly strong ipsilateral  evoked potentials where when we  stimulate the vcvs on the left we're  getting evoked potentials in the left  acc  when we stimulate  the vcbs on the right we sort of get the  same thing um acc on the right evoked  potentials  so  the  just to recap this so that  it's an interesting point of divergence  between the streamlines and the evoked  potentials in the dorsal anterior  cingulate  streamlines are anticipated  or are able to predict robust engagement  of the acc  most likely by means of the cingulum  bundle but we see virtually no evo  potentials in that structure  whereas the vcvs streamlines indicate  there are no fiber tracks directly  innervating the anterior cingulate but  we still see robust evoked potentials  that arise from stimulation to the vcbs  this double dissociation has interesting  implications just for our understanding  of prefrontal networks that are  modulated in dbs  single pulse stimulation can give rise  to action potentials as i mentioned in  both directions  and it can also drive firing of neural  assemblies through polysynaptic  innervation patterns that may not just  show up through the diffusion sensor  imaging itself  um  to our knowledge though this study  represents the first evidence of dorsal  acc being directly impacted  by ventral capsule ventral striatum  stimulation perhaps providing a novel  target for closed loop recording or  stimulation in future trials  overall our findings underscore the need  for deeper understanding in both  connectivity modalities particularly  focusing on reconciling the current  findings with an in-depth histological  study of the anterior cingulate to  demonstrate responsiveness to deep brain  stimulation  um going back to the bigger picture  using this multimodal integration we're  also able to sort of quantify the degree  of convergence between the predictions  of dti and the actuality of the applied  stimulation and corresponding evoked  potentials  so we see overall a quite strong  predictive relationship between the  streamlines and the evoked potentials in  our first two patients here indicated  as patient a and patient b when we look  at this segregated by stimulation target  we can actually see separation between  the scc and the vcbs in both patients  and now this is with all appropriate  caveats that you know only two patients  looking at the receiver operating  characteristics and the the nature of  the  differences in that predictive  relationship  we're not at this point able to  make any claims related to the  statistical significance or robustness  of this but it is certainly raising  questions for us and and contributing to  our um  ongoing hypothesis testing in this area  where we're seeing a sort of a stronger  predictive relationship  between lines and the evoked potentials  in the subclosal cingulate than we are  seeing in the ventral capsule ventral  striatum  and a step back even further um as a  final plug  just in the the scientific utility and  validity of the the stereo eeg approach  um you know the ongoing use of stereo  eeg and epilepsy  and its use  for intracranial research over the past  20 years  has produced a large body of  previously collected data which then  we're able to  cross compare with the new data that is  coming from the  the clinical trial in treatment  resistant depression so this is one of  the first applications of stereo aeg for  anything other than epilepsy  but it is not the case that we have no  depression sampling so epilepsy is  frequently comorbid with depression and  so because of that property and  similarity in the implantation locations  of the trd study with what  naturally clinically comes up in  patients with epilepsy  we're able to then build  our understanding and settle some really  old questions about the value of  intracranial recording in epilepsy  patients one of the questions was  what's the value of characterizing the  intracranial electrophysiology during  cognitive neuroscience tasks in patients  with epilepsy when we think they have  ongoing abnormalities in their  electrophysiology because of their  epilepsy and so we're able to  tease this apart a little bit because we  can do these recordings in similar  implantation  coverage  patients  in groups with epilepsy groups with  epilepsy plus depression and patients  with only depression and we can see  for example  uh indications of where the network  response is similar so in response on  the affective bias task to happy faces  we see a very similar response across  depression epilepsy plus depression and  just epilepsy  suggesting that the epilepsy  itself is not necessarily driving the  electrophysiological responses that  we're measuring in this model system  um  so overall the stereo eeg approach we're  very excited about it not necessarily as  a permanent  precursor to dbs for depression but as a  data and um  and hypothesis generating tool for the  future of science that really the data  that are collected in the sample of  patients in our current uh 3 study are  anticipated to inform our understanding  of the structure of the network for many  years to come  and we hope that this will advance over  time into an understanding of network  response to stimulation and optimization  of stimulation  that can result over time in less  invasive approaches for patients and  greater patient-specific optimization of  dbs for better scientific outcomes and  clinical outcomes  i just want to thank the members of my  lab brian metzger the postdoc maddie  mackey the phd student research  assistants for t calva and brian swee  who contributed tremendously to this  work  and the amazing baylor intracranial  neuromodulation group  and the  baylor intracranial neuromodulation  group led by samir schaff and wayne  goodman  including post-docs and graduate  students and research assistants  the neurosurgery fellows  the radiology staff  and  the many many people  and grant mechanisms that have supported  this work over the years and a specific  thank you to the um  pis of the uh three study dr chef  peradian and goodman and then the um the  key investigators cameron mcintyre jeff  cohn sanjay matthew and dave borton  thanks for your time  you

